#N canvas 390 134 503 449 de-jitter 0;
#X obj 68 95 tgl 16 0 empty \$0-set-dejitter empty 0 -8 0 10 #fcfcfc #000000 #000000 0 1;
#X msg 68 116 dejitter \$1;
#X obj 68 151 tgl 16 0 empty empty empty 0 -8 0 10 #fcfcfc #000000 #000000 0 1;
#X msg 68 172 debug-queue \$1;
#X obj 38 217 s \$0-in;
#X text 144 115 (de)activate de-jittering;
#X text 164 166 log de-jitter queue size (should ideally be as small as possible for minimum latency), f 43;
#X text 33 32 unfortunately \, the sensor creates bursts of messages at ~25Hz for higher rates [1]. this can be mitigated with de-jittering at the cost of latency., f 72;
#X text 33 269 Due to limitations of the Bluetooth communication protocol \, the sensor cannot transmit data in real-time after each data acquisition at sampling frequencies above 20Hz. Therefore \, the sensor uses a data packet transmission method \, meaning that multiple sets of data are acquired before being packaged and sent to the receiving end [...] \; \; Even if the sensor chip's sampling rate is 200Hz \, the Bluetooth module in the sensor cannot transmit 200Hz data back in real-time. It can only do so by packaging multiple data points into a single data packet and then sending it back to the receiving end. It's important to note that sensor data acquisition is real-time \, regardless of the sampling frequency (Hz) - it's just that data transmission is done through packetization., f 72;
#X text 33 252 [1] feedback from the WitSensor team on this:, f 72;
